Text matching, because alignment scores don't separate invented lines: on a 2 h Japanese film the outro blocks scored 0.07 to 0.82 against a median of 0.5 for the whole film. A prompt copy needs two different terms or one term three times, and no ! or ?, because a plain 'only glossary terms' rule dropped real shouts in an English cartoon ('Aang!', 'Toph! Toph!', 'Momo, Momo.'); single names and pairs are kept even when invented (e.g. 'लंका, लंका.'), preferring a missed invention to a lost line. Outro lines are matched against the whole block, so a real sentence containing one is kept. It runs in the segment stage rather than in transcribe, so existing transcripts are fixed with --force segment instead of hours of re-transcription; GLOSSARY is therefore a segment setting. The phrases live in invented.py, by language, so segment.py stays language-neutral and contributors add a language in one place; a module rather than a JSON file needs no package data for PyPI.
